# Lane G.3 (v1.3) — committed shell-completion scripts live under
# `target/completions/`. Lane G.8 (v1.3) — committed roff(7) man pages
# live under `target/man/`. The top-level target/ ignore is expressed as
# a children-only `/target/*` glob (not `/target/`) with explicit
# `!/target/completions/` + `!/target/man/` un-ignores. `/target/*` does
# NOT ignore the parent directory itself, which is what lets the un-ignores
# work — gitignore cannot re-include files inside a directory that is
# itself ignored. See:
# https://git-scm.com/docs/gitignore "It is not possible to re-include a file
# if a parent directory of that file is excluded."
#
# v1.3 Lane G fix-up R2 — honest-disclosure note (Codex R1 finding #5):
#
# Committing `target/completions/*` and `target/man/*` is a deliberate
# deviation from the Rust convention of treating `target/` as pure build
# artifact. The rationale is the `completion-drift` + `manpage-drift` CI
# gates: each PR rebuilds the binary and re-runs the generator, then
# `git diff` against the committed copies fails-fast when a contributor
# changes a flag without regenerating. Committing the artifact (instead
# of rebuilding it on every consumer install) lets operators inspect the
# exact bytes that ship in the `.deb`/`.rpm`/Homebrew bundle without
# needing a Rust toolchain.
#
# The cost is a small "build artifact in git" debt: every flag rename
# produces a +/- diff in `target/completions/` + `target/man/`. Reviewers
# should treat these diffs as derived state, not source; the source of
# truth is the clap CommandFactory tree in `crates/stealth-cli/src/`.
# The `cli-surface-drift` job (Lane G fix-up R2) is the upstream gate
# that fails first when the source-of-truth changes without the
# downstream artifacts being regenerated.
/target/*
!/target/completions/
!/target/man/
/_refs/
/vendor/*/target/
/vendor/*/.git/
Cargo.lock.bak
*.tmp
~/.rev_scraping/
.DS_Store

# soak runner local artifacts
/soak/
*.soak.log

# rev-auth / chromiumoxide test scratch dirs
/.tmp.*/
**/.tmp.*/

# Secrets — local environment files (never commit)
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
workspace/
.shared/
.claude/tmp/
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock
.claude/*.lock
.npm-cache/

# Node build artifacts (semantic-mcp-server)
node_modules/
# P10.5: top-level dist/ is globally excluded for build outputs, EXCEPT the
# hand-authored systemd deploy package which must ship with the repo.
# The bare `dist/` form would prevent nested un-ignore, so the top-level
# entries are listed explicitly and nested package dist/ trees are
# re-ignored under their specific package paths instead of with `**/dist/`
# (which would re-shadow `/dist/systemd`).
/dist/*
!/dist/systemd
# P7.1: hand-authored Hermes plugin scaffold ships with the repo for
# `rev-stealth hermes install` to consume.
!/dist/hermes
# Nested package build outputs.
scripts/semantic-mcp-server/dist/
build/

# P10.5: VPS deploy runbooks are not under dist/ but are listed here so
# future moves of dist-style ignore rules cannot accidentally shadow them.
!docs/deploy/
!docs/deploy/**
.semantic-mcp/*/semantic.db
.agent/project_id
.agent/tmp/
.agent/context/**
!.agent/context/.gitkeep
.agent/registry/*.jsonl
.agent-write-test-*

# v1.2.0: per-round Codex reviewer transcripts are large (8MB+) and ephemeral.
# Prompts (~256K) remain committed as scope evidence; reviews are excluded.
.agent/active/reviews/
.ask-readonly-*
harness-rust/target/
harness-rust/target
# NOTE: a broader `**/target/` glob used to live here; Lane G.3 removed it
# because gitignore cannot re-include files inside an ignored directory, and
# the broad glob silently re-shadowed the top-level
# `!/target/completions/` carve-out. All known nested `target/` dirs are
# enumerated explicitly above; add new ones explicitly if they appear.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.rlib
librust_out*
